const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Position vector, velocity vector, and speed\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eNewtonian mechanics set default space as a 3D space in which we live our daily life. Therefore, for any reference frame chosen in a 3D space, we can establish a relative/arbitrary coordinate system with a stable origin. The three axises are x-axis, y-axis, and z-axis, with each axis corresponds to one dimension.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThus, any point in this 3D space possesses its own position which can be represented as (x_{i}, y_{i}, z_{i}). Different points in space possess different positions, and the position can also be represented using a vector named position vector connecting the point in space and the origin of the coordinate system. Thus, for the same point with position (x_{i}, y_{i}, z_{i}), its corresponding position vector is expressed as: \\u0026lt;x_{i}, y_{i}, z_{i}\\u0026gt; (the origin is expressed as (0, 0, 0)).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSimilarly, we can have relative position without referring to the arbitrary coordinate system and its origin, this relative position difference is expressed as: \\u0026lt;x_{1}-x_{2}, y_{1}-y{2}, z_{1}-z_{2}\\u0026gt; (the origin is indeed a special case in which x_{2}, y_{2}, and z_{2} are zero). And if this represents the change of position from point 2 to point 1, then this vector is named as displacement vector--the change of position of vector (according to the subtraction rule of vectors, the result of subtraction is still a vector). In physics, we normally use /Delta to represent change (which is the same in mathematics), and thus displacements in x, y, z directions are represented as: /Deltax, /Deltay, and /Deltaz, respectively.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOne conclusion is that for every two random points in space, we can always have corresponding position vectors and displacement vectors. Notice that the displacement vector from point A to B is opposite to the one from point B to A since they are opposite/reversed operation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen, divide the displacement vector by time: since time is assumed as a scalar in Newtonian mechanics, the computed result is still a vector, and this contains the change of position (both the rate of change of position and rate of change of direction). Another clarification is that speed is different from velocity: speed is the magnitude of velocity so that it's a scalar only indicates the rate of change of position, but velocity also reflects direction. Applying calculus, velocity is the instantaneous rate of change of position vector (/overrightarrow{v}=\\\\lim{\\\\deltat \\\\to 0}\\\\frac{\\\\deltax}{\\\\deltat}=\\\\frac{dx}{dt}, in convention, when we're simplifying scenarios into one dimension, the default variable used is x). Thus, velocity is the instantaneous slope of position function with respect to time; speed diminishes the indication of direction.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd I guess this is information about position vector, velocity, and speed. I'll add more if I think of some useful yet omitted knowledge.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Kinematics\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/kinematics/\"}");
